    <item>
      <title>Bit parity counter (folding method) ..</title>
      <link>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/programming/c/bit_parity_counter_folding/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 27 Jul 2025 02:13:44 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/programming/c/bit_parity_counter_folding/</guid>
      <description>&lt;!-- markdownlint-disable MD033 --&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bit shifting is one of the most powerful tools in a low-level programmer&amp;rsquo;s toolbox. It allows direct manipulation of individual bits — a feature that shines in embedded systems, digital signal processing, and algorithm optimization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One common use of bitwise operations is checking &lt;strong&gt;parity&lt;/strong&gt; — whether the number of &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; bits in a binary representation is &lt;strong&gt;even&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;odd&lt;/strong&gt;. This can be useful for simple error detection, flag compression, or implementing certain logic gates in software.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
