    <item>
      <title>Installing Debian in a virtualized environment</title>
      <link>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/general/qemu/qemu_install/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/general/qemu/qemu_install/</guid>
      <description>&lt;!-- markdownlint-disable MD033 --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Need a safe sandbox to test drivers or experiments without risking your main OS? A virtual machine (VM) is a fast, safe way to do it with KVM/QEMU.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;prerequisites&#34;&gt;Prerequisites&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Debian/Ubuntu host with hardware virtualization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CPU virtualization enabled in BIOS/UEFI (Intel VT-x or AMD-V)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Quick checks:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;
&lt;table class=&#34;lntable&#34;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&#34;lntd&#34;&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lnt&#34;&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lnt&#34;&gt;2
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lnt&#34;&gt;3
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lnt&#34;&gt;4
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lnt&#34;&gt;5
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&#34;lntd&#34;&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-bash&#34; data-lang=&#34;bash&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;# CPU supports virtualization? (&amp;gt;0 means yes)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;egrep -c &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;(vmx|svm)&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; /proc/cpuinfo
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;lscpu &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -i virtualization &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nb&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;# Optional on Ubuntu: sudo apt install cpu-checker &amp;amp;&amp;amp; kvm-ok&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;h2 id=&#34;installation&#34;&gt;Installation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s update the system and install the necessary tools:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Logical / Arithmetic Right Shifts (c&#43;&#43;)</title>
      <link>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/programming/c&#43;&#43;/arithmeticlogicalshifting/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/programming/c&#43;&#43;/arithmeticlogicalshifting/</guid>
      <description>&lt;!-- markdownlint-disable MD033 --&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When working with bit manipulation in C++, understanding the difference between logical and arithmetic right shifts is crucial. This post explores both types of shifts using practical C++ examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-difference&#34;&gt;The Difference&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Logical Right Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: Always fills the leftmost bits with zeros, regardless of the original sign bit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;img-center&#34;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&#34;images/ArithmeticLogicShifting/zeroes.png&#34; alt=&#34;Logical Right Shift - Fill with zeros&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;img-caption&#34;&gt;Logic: Fill new bits with zeros&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arithmetic Right Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: Preserves the sign bit by filling the leftmost bits with the original sign bit value&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;img-center&#34;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&#34;images/ArithmeticLogicShifting/ones.png&#34; alt=&#34;Arithmetic Right Shift - Fill with sign bit&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;img-caption&#34;&gt;Arithmetic: Fill new bits with ones&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The leftmost bit is preserved, thus shifted bits are filled with ones.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Working with memory</title>
      <link>https://jlmunix.github.io/my-notes/posts/programming/c/workin_with_memory/</link>
